# 2. Ruby's historical position

Ruby appeared during an important period in programming-language development.

A simplified history looks like this:

```text
1950s
  │
  ├── FORTRAN ───── scientific computing
  │
1960s
  ├── ALGOL
  ├── Lisp ───────── functional / symbolic programming
  │
1970s
  ├── C ──────────── systems programming
  ├── Smalltalk ──── pure object-oriented programming
  │
1980s
  ├── C++
  ├── Perl ───────── scripting
  │
1990s
  ├── Python
  ├── Ruby
  ├── Java
  ├── JavaScript
  └── PHP
       │
       └── Internet / Web era
```

Ruby therefore belongs primarily to the **1990s scripting/Web era**.

It was influenced by several languages:

```text
             Ruby
               │
       ┌───────┼────────┐
       │       │        │
      Perl   Smalltalk Lisp
       │       │        │
   scripting   OOP   functional
```

Matz wanted Ruby to combine the strengths of several programming paradigms while keeping the language pleasant to use.

