# 3. Ruby is an object-oriented language

One of Ruby's most important characteristics is:

> **Everything is an object.**

For example:

```ruby
42.class
```

returns:

```text
Integer
```

And:

```ruby
"hello".class
```

returns:

```text
String
```

Even `nil` is an object:

```ruby
nil.class
```

You can call methods directly on values:

```ruby
5.times do
  puts "Hello"
end
```

This is a very Smalltalk-like idea.

In Ruby:

```ruby
5.times
```

means roughly:

> Send the `times` message to the object `5`.

---

# 4. Basic Ruby syntax

A Ruby program can be extremely simple:

```ruby
puts "Hello, Ruby!"
```

No:

```text
public static void main(...)
```

No semicolon is normally necessary.

Variables are also simple:

```ruby
name = "Mike"
age = 30

puts name
puts age
```

Ruby determines the type dynamically.

```ruby
x = 10
x = "hello"
```

This is legal.

Therefore Ruby is:

* dynamically typed
* strongly typed
* interpreted/JIT-compiled in modern implementations
* garbage collected
* object-oriented

---

# 5. Dynamic typing

Compare Java:

```java
int age = 30;
String name = "Mike";
```

Ruby:

```ruby
age = 30
name = "Mike"
```

You don't explicitly declare the variable's type.

You can write:

```ruby
x = 10
x = "hello"
x = [1, 2, 3]
```

The variable can refer to objects of different types during execution.

This is one of the major differences between Ruby and Java.

```text
Java
    variable
       │
       ▼
   declared type
       │
       ▼
     object

Ruby
    variable
       │
       ▼
     object
```

---

# 6. Everything is an expression

Ruby heavily emphasizes expressions.

For example:

```ruby
x = if age >= 18
      "adult"
    else
      "minor"
    end
```

The `if` itself produces a value.

Similarly:

```ruby
result = 10 > 5 ? "yes" : "no"
```

This makes Ruby code compact and expressive.

---

# 7. Classes

Ruby supports traditional object-oriented programming.

```ruby
class Student
  def initialize(name, age)
    @name = name
    @age = age
  end

  def introduce
    puts "I am #{@name}, age #{@age}"
  end
end
```

Create an object:

```ruby
student = Student.new("Alice", 20)

student.introduce
```

Output:

```text
I am Alice, age 20
```

Ruby's constructor is conventionally:

```ruby
initialize
```

and objects are created with:

```ruby
Student.new(...)
```

---

# 8. Instance variables

Ruby uses `@` for instance variables:

```ruby
class Student
  def initialize(name)
    @name = name
  end
end
```

So:

```ruby
@name
```

is an instance variable.

Ruby doesn't require Java-style declarations such as:

```java
private String name;
```

Instead, you can expose accessors:

```ruby
class Student
  attr_accessor :name
end
```

Then:

```ruby
student.name = "Alice"
puts student.name
```

This is a very Ruby-style feature.

---

# 9. Blocks

One of Ruby's most recognizable features is the **block**.

For example:

```ruby
[1, 2, 3].each do |number|
  puts number
end
```

You can also write:

```ruby
[1, 2, 3].each { |number| puts number }
```

The block is a piece of code passed to a method.

This makes Ruby extremely convenient for collections.

---

# 10. Enumerable and functional programming

Ruby supports many functional-programming concepts.

For example:

```ruby
numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]

squares = numbers.map { |n| n * n }
```

Result:

```ruby
[1, 4, 9, 16, 25]
```

Filtering:

```ruby
even_numbers = numbers.select { |n| n.even? }
```

Result:

```ruby
[2, 4]
```

Reducing:

```ruby
sum = numbers.reduce(0) { |total, n| total + n }
```

Result:

```text
15
```

This gives Ruby a strong functional-programming flavor even though Ruby is primarily considered an object-oriented language.

---

# 11. Duck typing

Ruby often uses **duck typing**.

The idea is:

> Don't care what an object *is*; care about what it *can do*.

Suppose you have:

```ruby
def print_name(object)
  puts object.name
end
```

Ruby doesn't require:

```text
object must implement interface Nameable
```

If the object responds to:

```ruby
name
```

it can work.

This is very different from Java's explicit interface-oriented style.

```text
Java:

"What type are you?"
        ↓
"Do you implement this interface?"
        ↓
"Then I can use you."

Ruby:

"Can you respond to name?"
        ↓
"Yes."
        ↓
"Then I can use you."
```

---

# 12. Metaprogramming

Ruby is famous for **metaprogramming**.

Programs can inspect and modify classes and objects dynamically.

For example:

```ruby
class Person
end

Person.define_method(:hello) do
  puts "Hello!"
end
```

Now:

```ruby
person = Person.new
person.hello
```

works.

Ruby provides mechanisms such as:

```ruby
define_method
send
method_missing
class_eval
instance_eval
```

This capability became extremely important in the Ruby on Rails ecosystem.

